description:
Source: CI wall-time analysis of 2026-09-27; Noah confirmed the institute's 20 self-hosted runners are idle; parent epic.

Every HiSim job runs on GitHub-hosted runners (`runs-on: ubuntu-latest`), which on the organisation's Free plan means 20 concurrent jobs shared with every other repository of FZJ-IEK3-VSA. Listing the self-hosted runners needs org admin (`gh api orgs/FZJ-IEK3-VSA/actions/runners` answers 403 for a member), so before any workflow can target them somebody with that right has to write down:

- the labels they carry (`self-hosted`, `linux`, and whatever custom label the fleet has), the runner group, and which repositories it is allowed for;
- cores and RAM per machine, and whether Docker is installed and usable by the runner user (every HiSim job runs in the `ghcr.io/fzj-iek3-vsa/hisim-test:py311` container, which needs Docker on a self-hosted runner);
- whether the machines share a filesystem or a cache directory (an `actions/cache` alternative for the inputs cache), and whether outbound access to ghcr.io and the UTSP is open.

Put the answer into this issue and into `.github/ci-monitoring.md`. The sibling issue moves the heavy jobs over once this is known.

Source: owner request 2026-10-14 (Noah), while deciding the mid-year oracle (hisim-midyear-windows-9g2.4).

Storages start from arbitrary or season-specific values today, and those starts produce simulation artefacts: transients of days in the stateful columns of every run, and different results depending on where a run starts. Measured inventory (roadmap/midyear_start_epic.md, "Initial states"):
- buffer / space-heating storage: hard-coded 35 °C, while its dataclass default says 25 °C (they disagree);
- DHW storage: 60 °C;
- house battery: state of charge 0 (empty);
- electric car battery: check its start value;
- hydrogen storage: 80 % full in one package and empty in the other;
- building thermal mass: 22 °C (not a storage charge; its field doubles as the comfort floor, hisim-component-physics-4g9.14);
- about 25 controllers start "off" with their minimum idle time blocking the first 15–60 minutes.

Owner direction: review the starting value of every storage and set it to about 50 % of full charge, so no run starts from an empty or full extreme.
- For an electrical or hydrogen storage: 50 % state of charge.
- For a water storage: halfway between its lower and upper operating temperature (define both per storage where they are not explicit; e.g. a DHW cylinder between its cold-water/return temperature and its set temperature).
- Keep one start value per storage, stated as a class constant with its unit and reason, and remove the contradiction between hard-coded and dataclass defaults.
- List every storage-like component (grep for state of charge, initial/start temperatures, fill levels) and record the old and new value per component in the PR.

Goldens move (every system with a storage); the change needs a CI bless with the per-pair moves reviewed. This supersedes the storage part of hisim-midyear-windows-9g2.2 (settle the contradictory defaults) and changes that plan's decision to keep the defaults. Done when: every storage starts at its stated ~50 % value, the defaults agree, tests pin the start values, and the golden moves are explained.

Source: evaluation of the review of #930, findings R1, R2, R8 and R10 (2026-10-13). Owner decision 2026-10-13: bead it, no fix now.

Mechanism: #930 (hisim-component-physics-4g9.52) evaluates hplib at the return itself where two 0.1 K grid points straddle hplib's switch to the heating rod alone. The predicate HplibResult.straddle_the_heating_rod_switch (hisim/components/more_advanced_heat_pump_hplib_model.py:497-513) compares `cop <= 1` of the two neighbours, and the COP it reads is the calibrated one: hplib_result_computed (more_advanced_heat_pump_hplib.py:2096) stores from_hplib(self.scop_calibration.apply(...)), and ScopCalibration.apply (…_model.py:412) leaves a rod-alone result at exactly 1.0 but divides a compressor result's electricity by the factor, so its calibrated COP is raw COP x factor. hplib itself switches where its raw COP reaches 1. With a factor below 1 the compressor grid point next to the switch reads a calibrated COP below 1 (raw 1.0033 x 0.876 = 0.879), both neighbours count as rod, the predicate returns False, and the code interpolates across the jump: the ramp #930 removed.

Failure case (probe on main 737f7801, 8 kW generic air/water, 0 °C, hot-water mode, return 64.05 °C, where hplib itself answers 8135.0 W):
- no stated SCOP: 8135.0 W;
- W35 4.0 and W55 3.0 (factors 0.840 / 0.881): 6467.1 W, the ramp;
- W55 3.0 alone (factor 0.876): 6467.1 W;
- W35 4.5 and W55 3.5 (factors 0.939 / 1.043): 8135.0 W.
The trigger is a W55 factor below 1, i.e. a stated W55 SCOP below the generic fit's own value (about 3.4 for this machine); many real air/water units are rated near 3. RenoVisor writes a request's heatpump_scop_en14825_w35 / _w55 onto the heat pump (hisim/renovisor/translate.py:1772-1773), and the air-source assembly takes both as parameters, so calibrated RenoVisor runs can get #930's slow loop back: a contraction of about 0.9 per pass, which needs more than the simulator's 100 passes at 60 s and aborts the run. The abort itself was not reproduced, only the probe. tests/test_heat_pump_heating_rod_switch.py builds its machine without a stated SCOP, so the calibration is a no-op there; with W55 = 3.0 its assertion answer == exact fails at 64.01, 64.05 and 64.09 °C.

Proposed fix: carry hplib's own verdict on the result, a field HplibResult.heating_rod_alone: bool set in hplib_result_computed from the raw results["COP"] <= HEATING_ROD_ALONE_MAXIMAL_COP before the calibration is applied; the predicate compares the two flags, and the calibrated COP stays for the booking. Use HEATING_ROD_ALONE_MAXIMAL_COP at every place that restates the threshold (…_model.py `if cop > 1`, `if cop <= 1`; review R18). Whether the calibration should also move the switch is the separate question hisim-z0v4.

Done when: the straddle is detected on hplib's raw verdict, and the rod-switch tests are parametrized over an uncalibrated and a W55 = 3.0 machine and both give hplib's exact answer at 64.01, 64.05 and 64.09 °C.

Owner, 2026-10-18, after looking at the Sankeys of a full-year heat-pump run (#1019): today they show only the heat pump, the one component of that house that declares energy ports; the buffer, the hot-water tank and the energy manager are grey 'undeclared' end nodes, and the heat pump's electricity appears to come from the energy manager.

This bead is the epic's acceptance: for every recorded twin (energy_systems/*.energy_system.yaml with a household), a full-year run writes
- an electricity Sankey from its sources (grid, PV, battery discharge) through the meters and the energy manager's dispatch to every consumer (appliances, heat pump or electric heater, car charging, battery charging) and to grid feed-in, with conversion and storage losses;
- a heat Sankey from its sources (fuel, ambient heat, sun on the collector, electricity turned into heat, district heat) through the generators, storages and distribution system to the building and the hot-water draw, with standby, distribution and combustion losses and the building's heat losses against its gains;
- an all-carriers Sankey joining the two where electricity becomes heat;
with no 'undeclared' node left (or each exemption named with its reason, hisim-energy-sankeys-9uoo.7) and the residual node (hisim-energy-sankeys-9uoo.9) at zero within tolerance.

Depends on: hisim-energy-sankeys-9uoo.3 (storages, distribution), hisim-energy-sankeys-9uoo.5 (electricity devices), hisim-energy-sankeys-9uoo.6 (building), hisim-energy-sankeys-9uoo.7 (the rest), hisim-energy-sankeys-9uoo.8 (system balance per carrier), hisim-energy-sankeys-9uoo.18 (links coloured by carrier, electricity drawn from its physical source rather than the energy manager), hisim-9w3g (display names on the nodes).

Done when: the Sankeys of every recorded twin show the whole house as above, a test asserts for one heat-pump twin and one boiler twin that no undeclared node remains, and the owner has reviewed the Sankeys of one twin per heating system.
